<div class="text-center">
	<h1>
		Summary (1983 - Paris - Algiers - Dakar)
	</h1>
	<p>
		<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1983/1983_map.jpg" style="float: right; height: 150px;"
			 alt="The route" />
		<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1983/1983_01.jpg" style="float: left; height: 166px;"
			 alt="" />The 1983 Dakar, which covered 10,000 km from 1 - 20 January, demanded
		a special kind of machine. On its way through Central Africa and across to the
		west coast of the magnificent continent, the drivers faced every obstacle imaginable,
		from stony tracks to the open, sandy Sahara. For Mitsubishi,
		the choice of vehicle for this epic high-speed journey through Africa
		was very simple: the Pajero - with its powerful engine and its astonishingly rugged
		four-wheel drive, it was the highlight of the high-performance off-roaders. The Pajero had debuted
		in Japan only a year earlier. It was entered in the 5th running of the Paris - Algiers - Dakar in
		standard trim, probably the greatest challenge for a road vehicle
		with only minor modifications. By day ten, Andrew Cowan and Colin Malkin
		had found their way to the top of the production and marathon class, and the eyes
		of the competition rested on Mitsubishi as
		<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1983//1983_02.jpg" style="float: right; height: 101px;"
			 alt="" />the newcomers stole the show from everyone. Sandstorms, rocks and extreme
		heat could do little to stop the steadfast Pajero, and although the
		Pajero ran in the marathon class, which meant that only very little on the car could be changed or
		modified, the Pajero rumbled home victorious. In the end,
		Cowan and Debussy, who piloted the second car, together with their Sonauto/Yamaha
		team, accepted the team prize, making the Pajero's first Dakar
		the greatest possible success.
	</p>
